e actually I was I wanted to do  theater and I wanted to be a theatre  actor and that's all I saw for you know  in terms of being an actor and and even  having made the film with Spielberg I  still wanted to be a you know wanted to  be a theatre actor so I came back to  London and carried on working in theater  and and was lucky enough to go off to to  do a couple of plays actually at the the  National Theatre not long after I got  back and so and that and then you know  but that's sort of balance you know I  guess once you've got a taste of that  sort of film language you know the it  became a question of wanting to really  have a balance and have both things and  go back and forth when I had started out  one of the main things for me was I  wanted to experience a lot of different  roles a lot of variety in what I was  doing and really explore the sort of  parameters of the craft in that way and  I think that was a really useful way of  beginning and then I mean things do  change you know over time and you become  more interested in maybe just a few more  specific things and really exploring  those you know the kind of narrow but  deep kind of way of looking at things  and and that is a useful thing as well  but I wouldn't start like that you know  I think I would start with trying if I  you know I think it was the right way to  begin to try and do as many different  things as possible to try and really  explore as much as possible because it  is a profession that allows you to  explore so many different worlds and  it's it's one of the great things about  it that you can go to all these  different spaces and so I was lucky  enough to be able to do some of that I  think that the first thing that you do  when you when you are looking at a part  that you're gonna play well for me  anyway is you work out it's sort of meta  isn't it it's like you work out how  you're going to think about the part you  know it's like you can't just apply  you know one size fits all meth